chunks.explanation_md:
Sometimes **rights and obligations arise from the same source**, for example a contract that gives each party rights and obligations. If they are **interdependent and cannot be separated**, they form a **single inseparable asset or liability**: one unit of account (as with executory contracts). If rights are **separable** from obligations, it may be appropriate to group them separately, giving separate assets and liabilities, or to group them together as a single asset or liability.

Treating a set of rights and obligations as one unit is **different from offsetting** assets and liabilities.

**Possible units of account:**

(a) an individual right or obligation;
(b) all rights, all obligations, or all of both, from a single source such as a contract;
(c) a subgroup, for example a subgroup of rights over an item of property, plant and equipment with a different useful life or pattern of consumption;
(d) a group from a portfolio of similar items;
(e) a group from a portfolio of dissimilar items, for example items to be disposed of in one transaction; and
(f) a risk exposure within a portfolio, where the portfolio has a common risk.
